[38;2;124;124;124m**Key identity (Lucas' theorem).** For $i \ge 0$, write $i$ in binary as $i = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\sum_{k \in S} 2^k$ where $S$ is the set of positions of 1-bits, and let $s(i) =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m|S|$. In $\mathbb{F}_2[x]$,[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$(1+x)^i = \prod_{k \in S}(1 + x^{2^k}),$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mso $o(Q_i) = 2^{s(i)}$. In particular, $s(2m) = s(m)$ and $s(2m+1) = s(m)+1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Decomposition.** For $i \ge 1$, write $i = 2m + r$ with $r \in \{0,1\}$. In $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{F}_2[x]$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $r = 0$: $(1+x)^{2m} = (1+x^2)^m$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $r = 1$: $(1+x)^{2m+1} = (1+x)(1+x^2)^m$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mGiven $0 \le i_1 < \dots < i_n$, let $m_j = \lfloor i_j/2 \rfloor$. Define:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$A(y) = \sum_{j: i_j \text{ even}} (1+y)^{m_j}, \qquad B(y) = \sum_{j: i_j [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{ odd}} (1+y)^{m_j}.$$[0m

