[38;2;124;124;124mxB(x^2)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mEven-degree coefficients of $R$ come from $(A+B)(x^2)$; odd-degree coefficients [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfrom $xB(x^2)$. These are disjoint, so:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$o(R) = o(A+B) + o(B). \tag{$\star$}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Cancellation structure.** Since the $i_j$ are strictly increasing, the $m_j$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare non-decreasing. We have $m_j = m_{j+1}$ iff $\{i_j, i_{j+1}\} = \{2m, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2m+1\}$ for some $m$. For each value $m$ appearing among the $m_j$, let $c_m$ be[0m[38;2;124;124;124mits multiplicity ($c_m \in \{1,2\}$). In $\mathbb{F}_2$, terms with $c_m = 2$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcancel. Define:[0m
