[38;2;124;124;124m- $S_2 = \{m : c_m = 2\}$ — both $2m, 2m+1$ are chosen.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $S_1^{\text{even}} = \{m \in S_1 : 2m \in \{i_j\}\}$, $S_1^{\text{odd}} = \{m [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\in S_1 : 2m+1 \in \{i_j\}\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThen $A + B = \sum_{m \in S_1}(1+y)^m$ and $B = \sum_{m \in S_1^{\text{odd}} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\cup S_2}(1+y)^m$, both sums over **distinct** non-negative integers.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Induction.** We prove by induction on $i_1$ that $o(\sum_{j=1}^n Q_{i_j}) \ge [0m[38;2;124;124;124mo(Q_{i_1})$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m*Base case $i_1 = 0$:* $o(Q_0) = 1$. The sum $R$ has leading term $x^{i_n}$ with[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcoefficient 1 (no other $Q_{i_j}$ has degree $i_n$), so $R \neq 0$ and $o(R) \ge[0m[38;2;124;124;124m1 = o(Q_0)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m*Inductive step ($i_1 \ge 1$):* Assume the result holds whenever the smallest [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mindex is $< i_1$. Let $m_1 = \lfloor i_1/2 \rfloor < i_1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Case 1: $i_1 = 2m_1$ even.** Then $o(Q_{i_1}) = 2^{s(m_1)}$.[0m

