[38;2;124;124;124m$m_j$). By the induction hypothesis applied to $\sum_{m \in S_1}(1+y)^m$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(smallest index $m_1 < i_1$):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$o(A+B) \ge o((1+y)^{m_1}) = 2^{s(m_1)} = o(Q_{i_1}).$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBy $(\star)$, $o(R) = o(A+B) + o(B) \ge o(Q_{i_1})$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- *Subcase 1b: $c_{m_1} = 2$* (i.e., $i_2 = 2m_1+1$). Then $m_1 \in S_2$, and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$m_1$ is the smallest element of $S_1^{\text{odd}} \cup S_2$. By induction [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mapplied to $B = \sum_{m \in S_1^{\text{odd}} \cup S_2}(1+y)^m$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$o(B) \ge o((1+y)^{m_1}) = 2^{s(m_1)} = o(Q_{i_1}).$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBy $(\star)$, $o(R) = o(A+B) + o(B) \ge o(Q_{i_1})$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Case 2: $i_1 = 2m_1+1$ odd.** Then $o(Q_{i_1}) = 2^{s(m_1)+1}$. Since $i_1$ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe smallest index, $2m_1 \notin \{i_j\}$, so $c_{m_1} = 1$ and $m_1 \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mS_1^{\text{odd}} \subset S_1$. The value $m_1$ is the smallest element of both [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$S_1$ and $S_1^{\text{odd}} \cup S_2$. By induction applied to both sums [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(smallest index $m_1 < i_1$):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$o(A+B) \ge 2^{s(m_1)}, \qquad o(B) \ge 2^{s(m_1)}.$$[0m
